  "metadata": {
    "alignment": "standard",
    "description": "eugr's (Eugene Rakhmatulin) spark-vllm-docker gemma4-26b-a4b-nvfp4 recipe for NVIDIA's Gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4 (ModelOpt NVFP4, Apache-2.0; checkpoint revision a19cfe00) with Google's gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-assistant MTP drafter (revision 6e5aaaf4), served by vLLM on a single Spark. Runs eugr's published vllm-node image (nightly-20260929, digest pinned; its build-metadata records spark-vllm-docker 42f62e34, vLLM 0.30.1rc1.dev336+gaf5b4857e and FlashInfer 4a638133) with upstream's launch: InstantTensor loading, FP8 KV cache, prefix caching, gemma4 reasoning and tool-call parsers, MTP speculative decoding with four tokens and a Triton drafter MoE backend, 262,144-token context, 8,192 batched tokens and memory utilisation 0.7 (upstream defaults); the sequence limit is left to vLLM's default as upstream sets none. The target weights mount at /models/target and the drafter at /models/assistant, so the speculative-config model is the mounted drafter path instead of upstream's Hugging Face id. Upstream's recipe is solo-only (tensor parallel 1 here; its tensor_parallel default of 2 and --distributed-executor-backend ray are not used in solo mode). No upstream performance claims are recorded. Upstream repository MIT. Physical Spark acceptance is pending. GB10 memory correction: Per-node peak budgets utilization × 130,663,231,488-byte unified memory plus at least 8 GB for CUDA context/graphs, FlashInfer workspace, auxiliary heads and CPU/mmap buffers; 8 GB reserve, with a separate 6 GB OS floor. This is a conservative budget, not a measured footprint.",
    "tags": [
      "candidate",
      "eugr",
      "executable",
      "gemma",
      "gemma-4",
      "mtp",
      "nvfp4",
      "reasoning",
